From b06a77fc2f3b8fdf9fdf72b37a225e03afe1d239 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:19:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 61/61] inline-3d(Phase 1): key GPU weave state by element
 identity, not by index
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every per-target resource on the GPU thread — the provider slot (and so
its keyed-mutex weave input), the B4d scratch image, the imported woven
mailbox, the in-flight submit rect — was addressed by the target's INDEX
in inline_3d_rects_. That is only correct while the vector is stable, and
it is not. A tile appearing, disappearing, or reordering shifted every
later target's index, and each one silently inherited a different
element's state for a frame or more: submits landing in the wrong
keyed-mutex input, scratch images sized for the wrong rect, the
"pending" rect of one tile promoted as another's woven rect. The
previous patch's id-primary sort stops the reordering half; this removes
the class.

DisplayXRTargetState now holds all of it, in a
base::flat_map<base::Token, ...> keyed by the element's
TrackedElementId, and the two index-keyed vectors plus the
never-populated woven-state vector are gone. Rects with no identity (the
legacy main-thread metadata channel) key positionally under a synthetic
Token(0, index), which reproduces exactly the old behaviour for that
channel — its order is stable and purely geometric — while keeping each
legacy rect on a distinct key rather than collapsing them onto the zero
token. A derived id always has a random non-zero high word, so the two
key spaces cannot collide.

Reconciliation replaces the old "resize the parallel vectors to the rect
count":
  - targets absent from a frame are aged, not dropped. 30 frames (~0.5 s)
    of grace, because a tile can miss a frame for reasons unrelated to
    going away — a canvas resource not ready, a re-raster, a lost race
    during a fling — and rebuilding its shared images next frame is a
    hitch plus a visible cold start. Off-window tiles never enter this
    path at all: they still appear in inline_3d_rects_ and are only
    intersected to empty in the submit loop, so scrolling away and back
    costs nothing.
  - new targets take the lowest unused provider slot. The provider's
    target_id is browser-internal (it never crosses the weave mojo
    boundary — SubmitBatch ships rect tuples), so dense allocation and
    reuse are safe and keep the provider's per-target pools bounded.
  - a hard cap of 32 live targets with LRU eviction and a one-shot
    warning, so a page cycling canvases degrades instead of exhausting
    the device.
  - prune calls the provider BEFORE releasing the slot, or the next
    target admitted inherits the previous one's un-taken result handles
    and stale keyed-mutex input.

That single-target retirement is new provider API: PruneTarget(uint32_t).
PruneTargets(size_t)'s [0, count) form assumed ids were dense and
positional and cannot express "id 3 retires, 4 and 5 stay". The Windows
provider closes that id's result handles and resets its input; the mac
provider owns no per-target handles (one shared input, one cached woven
IOSurface) so it just drops the stored result.

Also id-first: canvas_mailbox_for() pairs a target with its clean-SBS
resource by token — the aggregator already worked out which resource
belongs to which element — and only falls back to the 70%-overlap test
for identity-less rects, logging a throttled "GPU canvas-layer id miss"
when a stamped id finds no resource.

A DCHECK asserts the keys are unique. Duplicate tokens would share one
target and the second submit would silently overwrite the first; the
aggregator collapses duplicates before the frame ships, so a failure
here means that collapse regressed.

